// smallcaps-demo — small caps two ways. Latin Modern and Alegreya carry a dedicated small-caps cut, drawn
// directly. EB Garamond has no separate small-caps file, but its regular cut carries the OpenType smcp
// feature, so the engine synthesizes small caps from its lowercase letters. Both go through \textsc.

\use{document}

\title{Small Capitals}
\author{The texish engine}
\date{Version 0.19.9}
\maketitle

\section{A drawn small-caps cut (Latin Modern)}

The body face here is Latin Modern, which ships a real small-caps font. \textsc{The quick brown fox jumps
over the lazy dog} — set with \verb|\textsc|, the lowercase letters come from the small-caps cut and the
capitals from the roman.

\section{Synthesized small caps (EB Garamond)}

EB Garamond has no separate small-caps file. Its roman does carry the OpenType \verb|smcp| feature, so the
engine synthesizes small caps from the lowercase letters:

\medskip

{\font ebgaramond 12
\textsc{The quick brown fox jumps over the lazy dog.} The capital letters stay full height and the rest drop
to small capitals, all from the one roman font.
}

